# The DECLARED lane, in a project `sig/` — the one lane graded as an EXACT
# match, because it is copied from the author's annotation rather than inferred
# (ADR-0043 § 2; upstream ADR-103 WD17 rules the declared lane is never judged).
#
# This fixture is also the only project-`sig/` coverage a standing gate has:
# every other corpus tool in this repo runs both sides from a clean cwd with no
# config, so no `sig/` is ever loaded.
#
# WHY THE ORACLE SURFACES `declared:` FOR EXACTLY ONE OF THE THREE ANNOTATED
# METHODS BELOW — solved 2026-08-26, see
# `docs/notes/20260826-effects-s1-catalogue-probe.md` § 7 (five predictions
# written before the run, all five confirmed) and
# `docs/notes/20260826-effects-s6-probe.md` § 1-3, which extends it.
#
# **`declared:` is the CALLER's lane.** An envelope on a method never colours
# that method's own row. `UnitScan#import_envelope` (`unit_scan.rb:328-338`)
# runs while scanning a CALL: if the callee's declaration carries an envelope,
# the bound joins the *caller's* `≤` lane under an `envelope:` origin, and it
# then travels call edges like the proven lane (`propagator.rb:137`).
#
# A second rule stacks on top at render time:
# `Entry#rendered_declared = declared.excluding_subsumed_by(proven)`
# (`effect_table.rb:41-43` → `label_set.rb:100-107`) — a declared label the
# proven lane already admits is dropped from the output, over the TRANSITIVE
# lanes of both, and the relation is asymmetric (a proven ANCESTOR drops a
# declared descendant; a proven descendant does not drop a declared ancestor).
#
# That explains this fixture with no residue: `load_and_log` CALLS `load_row`,
# so it carries `load_row`'s `io.db`; `load_row` itself calls nothing annotated,
# so its own lane is empty; `formats` is `%a{pure}` — the EMPTY bound, which
# `add_declared` early-returns on (`unit_scan.rb:156`) — and is uncalled;
# `unannotated` has nothing.
#
# So the port's slice 6 owes TWO rules, not one: (a) an envelope on a callee
# joins the CALLER's declared lane, keyed `envelope:<Owner>#<sel>`, and
# propagates transitively; (b) the rendered value subtracts anything the proven
# lane admits. Omitting (b) alone is a DECLARED-MISMATCH on correct code — and
# because the port's proven lane is DIRECT while upstream's is transitive,
# (b) breaks in BOTH directions today (s6 probe § 3c). Until that is fixed the
# port reports `methods: {}` for any project that could produce a lane at all
# (`crates/rigor-cli/src/effects/mod.rs`'s `carries_effect_annotations`), which
# is why this fixture scores four `absent-method` UNDERs and no mismatch.

class Declared
  # `%a{pure}` is the ecosystem's existing purity annotation, read as the EMPTY
  # envelope (WD5 surface 3).
  %a{pure}
  def formats: (Integer) -> String

  # The v1 spelling of a bounded envelope: this method may touch the database
  # and nothing else.
  %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db}
  def load_row: (Integer) -> String

  # An envelope whose body EXCEEDS it — the shape `effect.envelope-exceeded`
  # fires on once the diagnostic half lands (port slice 6). Until then this is
  # here so the declared lane has a case where the two lanes disagree.
  %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db}
  def load_and_log: (Integer) -> String

  # No annotation at all: the declared lane must be empty on both sides.
  def unannotated: () -> void
end
